s goings.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ*There is no darkness, nor shadow of death,¦layout ‚¤textÙ2Where the workers of iniquity may hide themselves.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ-For he will not lay upon man more than right;¦layout ‚¤textÙ,That he should enter into judgment with God.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ3He shall break in pieces mighty men without number,¦layout ‚¤text¾And set others in their stead.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ!Therefore he knoweth their works,¦layout ‚¤textÙAAnd he overturneth them in the night, so that they are destroyed.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤text¾He striketh them as wicked men¦layout ‚¤text¼In the open sight of others;¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ"Because they turned back from him,¦layout ‚¤textÙ'And would not consider any of his ways:¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ8So that they cause the cry of the poor to come unto him,¦layout ‚¤textÙ(And he heareth the cry of the afflicted.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines“‚¤textÙ4When he giveth quietness, who then can make trouble?¦layout ‚¤textÙ5And when he hideth his face, who then can behold him?¦layout§quote_2‚¤textÙ;Whether it be done against a nation, or against a man only:¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤text½That the hypocrite reign not,¦layout ‚¤text¼Lest the people be ensnared.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter"«first_verseªlast_verse!¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines’‚¤textÙ&Surely it is meet to be said unto God,¦layout ‚¤textÙ6I have borne chastisement, I will not offend any more:¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ#That which I see not teach thou me:¦layout ‚¤textÙ+If I have done iniquity, I will do no more.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse „¥verse!¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines”‚¤textÙ#Should it be according to thy mind?¦layout ‚¤textÙ+He will recompense it, whether thou refuse,¦layout§quote_2‚¤textÙ"Or whether thou choose; and not I:¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ"Therefore speak what thou knowest.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse!…¤book§chapter"«first_verse"ªlast_verse%¦verses”„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines’‚¤textÙ!Let men of understanding tell me,¦layout ‚¤textÙ#And let a wise man hearken unto me.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse"„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ"Job hath spoken without knowledge,¦layout ‚¤textÙ"And his words were without wisdom.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse#„¥verse$¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ/My desire is that Job may be tried unto the end¦layout ‚¤textÙ&Because of his answers for wicked men.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse$„¥verse%¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines“‚¤textÙ%For he addeth rebellion unto his sin,¦layout ‚¤text¿He clappeth his hands among us,¦layout§quote_2‚¤textÙ&And multiplieth his words against God.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse%